+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same plot visit independently. Twenty-four seasonal workers share twelve tablets across 320 permanent plots, producing about 6,000 visit records a season, and a tablet can be offline for 48 hours. Two workers who compare field notes at the end of a day may each edit the same visit from the same starting state; one of them submits against a state the other has already superseded. A regional science partner receives a reviewed CSV every Friday.
+
+The record store must therefore define what a visit's current state *is* when independently edited copies arrive. Getting this wrong is expensive in a way the team feels late: an edit silently dropped, or two edits silently fused into a record no observer actually made, reaches the partner as data. Nobody notices at submission time, and the worker who could have caught it has moved to another site.
+
+A visit's scientific fields are observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some concurrent edits are plainly unrelated — correcting a misspelled observer name while another worker adds a species that was missed. Others are related in ways only a botanist sees: revising a species identification changes what its cover estimate means. Nothing in the field names distinguishes these two situations.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gate: no submitted edit is discarded without a human act
+
+Inherited from the tenet *do not silently discard a submitted edit*. The system being replaced fails exactly here — spreadsheet imports overwrite corrections without showing which submission was lost. An option that resolves concurrency by preferring one submission over another on any automatic basis is eliminated, not scored down.
+
+### Criterion: scientifically related edits are never combined without a human noticing — major
+
+The HLD makes *inference of scientific intent* a non-goal. A policy that combines field values on its own is making exactly that inference whenever the fields it combines are scientifically coupled. The failure is silent and produces a plausible-looking record, which is worse than a visible backlog: a revised identification carrying the previous estimate's cover value is a record that reads as valid and is wrong.
+
+### Criterion: review workload two coordinators can carry before Friday — major
+
+From the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. The load depends on how often concurrent edits actually occur, which is not known for this team; the shared-tablet pattern and the end-of-day note comparison suggest contests cluster rather than spread evenly, so a weekly peak matters more than a season average. A queue that cannot be cleared by Friday converts into either a late export or a rubber-stamped one.
+
+### Criterion: an exported value is explainable back to a submission — moderate
+
+From the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. When the partner questions a number, a coordinator has to be able to say where it came from and who decided it.
+
+### Criterion: implementation and ongoing maintenance burden — moderate
+
+Weighted for ongoing cost, not just initial build. Logic that encodes assumptions about the field set has to be revisited whenever the field set changes, and this team has no systems administrator to own that.
+
+### Criterion: tablet submission size and local storage — moderate
+
+Twelve shared tablets accumulating up to 48 hours of unsent work, on hardware the team already owns.
+
+### Criterion: how many visits are export-eligible on Friday — minor
+
+Visits held out of the export are not lost, only late; the partner's columns are unchanged either way. Minor because a deferred visit is recoverable and a wrong one is not.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** A visit is a graph of revisions, each holding a complete set of the visit's scientific fields. Every submission names the revision it was edited from. If that base is the visit's only current revision, the new revision supersedes it and the visit stays settled. If the base has already been superseded, the submission is still accepted and becomes a second current revision: the visit is now contested and has no single current state. A coordinator resolves a contest deliberately, either adopting one competing revision as written or composing a field set after reading them both. Resolution adds a revision whose bases are the revisions it resolved; nothing is edited or removed.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers both edit from revision `r4`. One corrects an observer name, the other adds *Salicornia pacifica* at 12% cover. Both submissions are accepted; `r5` and `r6` are both current and the visit is contested. A coordinator sees both whole visits against their shared baseline `r4`, composes a field set carrying the corrected observer *and* the added species, and submits it as `r7` with both revisions as its bases. The visit is settled, and `r5` and `r6` remain readable.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Gate — **passes.** Every submission becomes a durable revision. The only way a submitted value fails to reach the export is a coordinator reading it and deciding otherwise.
+- Related edits never combined silently — **strong.** The server performs no field-level combination at all, so the failure mode does not exist in the mechanism. Combination happens only in a composed resolution, which is a person's act on two whole visits they have read.
+- Review workload — **weak.** Every concurrent edit produces review work regardless of whether the edits touch the same field. The observer-name-plus-new-species case above needs a coordinator even though no field overlaps.
+- Explainable export — **strong.** The revision graph gives the exported value a single current revision, with its author and receipt time, and any resolution names the coordinator and the revisions they resolved.
+- Implementation and maintenance — **strong.** The server compares a base reference against the set of current revisions; it holds no knowledge of what the scientific fields mean, so adding or changing a field changes nothing about concurrency.
+- Submission size and local storage — **partial.** Each submission carries the complete field set, and each draft retains a base revision ID. Visit records are small structured documents with no photographs, so complete-payload submission is affordable.
+- Export-eligible on Friday — **weak.** Contested visits are held out of the CSV until resolved, including contests over unrelated fields.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger coordinator queue for a server that never interprets the data it stores.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies the base state it was edited from and the changes it makes. When two edits touch disjoint fields, the server combines them into a single current record automatically and no coordinator is involved. When they touch the same field, the overlap goes to review. The server maintains per-field combination rules: scalars like observation date are single-valued, the observer list is a set union, species cover estimates are keyed entries where an addition and an unrelated revision can coexist. Field-level provenance records which submission each surviving value came from.
+
+**Demonstration.** The same two workers edit from the same base. The observer-name correction and the *Salicornia pacifica* addition touch different fields, so the server combines them: one current record, no review work, export-eligible immediately. The coordinator's queue holds only genuine overlaps.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Gate — **passes.** Original submissions are retained, and overlapping changes go to a coordinator rather than being resolved by preference.
+- Related edits never combined silently — **weak.** Disjoint *fields* are not the same as unrelated *observations*. A worker revising a species identification and a second worker adjusting that species' cover estimate, each from the same base, produce changes the rules see as separable and combine into a record neither worker submitted. No rule set expressible over field names detects this, and the combined record reaches the export looking ordinary.
+- Review workload — **strong.** Only real field overlaps queue. Unrelated corrections clear without a coordinator, which is the common case the shared-tablet pattern produces.
+- Explainable export — **partial.** Field-level provenance explains where each value came from, but the explanation is per-field and the exported record corresponds to no single submission. A coordinator answering the partner describes an assembly rather than a decision.
+- Implementation and maintenance — **weak.** Every field needs a combination rule, the list-valued and keyed-entry rules are the hard ones, and the rules must be revisited whenever the field set changes. There is no systems administrator to own that revisiting.
+- Submission size and local storage — **strong.** Structured changes against a retained base snapshot are smaller than complete field sets.
+- Export-eligible on Friday — **strong.** More visits settle without review, so more reach the weekly CSV.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a rule set that can silently combine scientifically coupled edits for a much smaller review queue.
+
+### Most-recent submission wins
+
+**Description.** The server stores every submission for audit, and the visit's current state is whichever submission arrived last. No review work, no base tracking, no merge rules.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Gate — **eliminated.** Arrival order decides which observations reach the partner, and the losing edit is dropped with no human reading it. Retaining the superseded submission in an audit log does not satisfy the tenet; it reproduces the current spreadsheet failure with better records of it.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the major criteria pulling opposite ways, and on which failure the team can recover from. Field-aware merge wins decisively on review workload — the criterion the tenet about two coordinators engages — and it wins on export completeness and submission size. Whole-visit revisions win on the other major criterion, and that criterion carries a failure the team cannot detect: a record assembled from two scientifically coupled edits looks exactly like a correct record, and the people who could recognize it are in the field. The review-workload cost is visible, bounded, and recoverable — a contested visit waits a week.
+
+This is taken knowingly against the lean of *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*, and it is taken for *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and for the HLD's non-goal of inferring scientific intent. A reviewed record that one person decided is more defensible to the science partner than an assembled one whose rules nobody owns.
+
+**What this forecloses.** No automatic combination of concurrent edits, including edits to plainly unrelated fields. No field-level provenance: the record store tracks provenance at whole-revision granularity, so a question about one value is answered by naming the revision and the coordinator, not by attributing that field to a submission. A coordinator who wants both workers' corrections composes a field set by hand.
+
+**What this obligates downstream.** Both obligations are cascades into sibling nodes, which size their own designs around them:
+
+- **tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID for every local draft and every pending submission, so an edit made offline still names the state it came from 48 hours later. It submits complete field sets rather than change lists. It treats `accepted_contested` as a success, telling the worker their observations are stored and a coordinator is reconciling them — a field worker with no connectivity and nobody to ask must not see this as an error. It cannot assume a submission makes its own edit the current state, and it must handle a pre-resolution revision it still holds becoming non-current.
+- **review-export** gains a review queue of contested visits as first-class work, not an error list. It needs a revision comparison view built on the contested retrieval response and its server-computed divergence point, showing each competing whole visit against the shared baseline. It offers both resolution forms, including composition, and sends the coordinator's expected current-revision set so a stale resolution fails rather than applying to a contest they never read. Contested visits are held out of the reviewed CSV until settled, which makes queue age before Friday something this node has to make visible.
+
+**What would reverse this.** Measured contest volume that two coordinators cannot clear in a week, or a pattern where the great majority of contests are provably unrelated single-field corrections. The reversal is not symmetric, though: reconsidering means accepting automatic combination of coupled edits, so the condition to watch is whether the team gains someone to own merge rules — not merely whether the queue is long.
